  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The bulk of a typical gasoline consists of hydrocarbons with between <math > <mn>4</mn></math> and <math > <mrow>  <mn>12</mn></mrow></math> carbon atoms per molecule (commonly referred to as <math > <mrow>  <mtext>C4</mtext><mo>&#x2212;</mo><mtext>C12</mtext></mrow></math>). It is a mixture of paraffins (alkanes), cycloalkanes (naphthenes), and olefins (alkenes), where the usage of the terms paraffin and olefin is particular to the oil industry. The actual ratio depends on:</p><ul>\t<li>The oil refinery that makes the gasoline, as not all refineries have the same set of processing units;</li>\t<li>The crude oil feed used by the refinery;</li>\t<li>The grade of gasoline, in particular, the octane rating.</li></ul><p>Below carbon atoms per molecule are mentioned.</p><p><strong>(i)</strong> Gasoline <math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></msub></math><br /><br /><strong>(ii) </strong>Kerosene <math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>13</mn></mrow></msub></math><br /><br /><strong>(iii)</strong> Gas oil <math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>13</mn></mrow></msub><mo>-</mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>18</mn></mrow></msub></math></p>",
    "image": null
  },
